/*
 * **Engineering options** — which blueprints and which experimental effects each
 * outfitting module can actually take.
 *
 * Sources: EDSY (github.com/taleden/EDSY) eddb.js — its module-group tables carry the
 *          `blueprints` and `expeffects` a group offers, and mark the individual
 *          modules that accept no experimental at all. EDSY is (c) taleden under a
 *          CC BY-NC 4.0 License (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/).
 *          The underlying game logic is Elite Dangerous data, property of Frontier
 *          Developments plc, under Frontier's media-usage terms:
 *          https://forums.frontier.co.uk/threads/510879/
 *
 * Availability is a property of the **module**, not of the blueprint: a Pulse Laser and
 * a Rail Gun share the Efficient blueprint but offer different experimental effects. So
 * modules are grouped (`modules` maps a module symbol to a `groups` key) and each group
 * lists the `blueprints` and `experimentals` it offers. Both lists hold ids that join
 * straight to `blueprints.jsonc` and `experimental-effects.jsonc`.
 *
 * `exclusions` handles the modules that are the exception to their group: a Multi-cannon
 * cannot take Phasing Sequence, and the mining tools take no experimental at all. Each
 * entry lists the effect ids that module cannot take, already expanded — subtract it
 * from the group's `experimentals` to get the exact set. Modules absent from
 * `exclusions` take their whole group's list.
 *
 * Only modules the game lets you engineer appear at all; everything else is absent.
 *
 * Full provenance: data/ships/SOURCES.md.
 */{
